From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 15:47:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] fix(gastown): repair queue-starvation-check, which could
 never fire
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The deacon's queue-starvation-check step was dead code. Four independent
defects stacked, each sufficient on its own to make every session report a
zero-length queue — so the step that exists to catch "active polling, wrong
filter, queue silently piling up" was itself doing exactly that.

1. `gc agents list` is not a command. It fails with json_command_not_found,
   the piped jq gets an error object, the `while read` loop body never runs,
   and the pipeline still exits 0.
2. The per-alias prefilter `--assignee="$AGENT"` assumes one identity form.
   A session is addressed by at least three depending on what assigned the
   work: agent alias (molecule wisps), session bead id (mail), and session
   name (polecat claims). Any single guess reports zero.
3. `--include-infra` was absent, so wisps, molecules, sessions and messages —
   nearly everything a coordination session holds — were invisible.
4. The hq/city store is not addressable as `gc bd --rig=<name>`; that form
   errors with `rig "<name>" not found` and exits 0, silently contributing an
   empty queue for the entire city.

Replaces the per-alias prefilter with an identity-agnostic join: read the
actual assignee strings out of the bead data, then match each active session
on any of its identity forms. Verified against a live 6-session city — the
old query returned 0 for every session; the new one returns deacon 3,
refinery 3, witness 2, and the witness count only lands because the join
catches a mail bead assigned by session bead id.

Also surfaces unmatched assignees as orphaned work in the same pass.

Refs: gc-8gh (defect 1), gc-98n (defect 2)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 gastown/formulas/mol-deacon-patrol.toml | 42 +++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 36 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/gastown/formulas/mol-deacon-patrol.toml b/gastown/formulas/mol-deacon-patrol.toml
index 39e3144..357d522 100644
--- a/gastown/formulas/mol-deacon-patrol.toml
+++ b/gastown/formulas/mol-deacon-patrol.toml
@@ -205,15 +205,45 @@ closes that gap.
 - `A` = count of open beads assigned to the session's agent identity
 - `B` = the most recent bead.updated_at across those beads (work signal)
 
+Read the ACTUAL assignee strings out of the bead data and join them against
+every identity form a session carries — never pre-filter with
+`--assignee=<one guessed identity>`. A session is addressed by at least three
+different strings depending on what assigned the work: the agent alias
+(`gastown.deacon`, on molecule wisps), the session bead id (`gc-ttk`, on mail
+messages), and the session name (`gastown__polecat-gc-qkw`, on polecat
+claims). Filtering by any single one silently reports zero — which is the
+very failure mode this step exists to catch.
+
+Two more things this query gets right, both of which returned a flat zero for
+every session before:
+- `--include-infra` is REQUIRED. Wisps, molecules, sessions and messages live
+  in the wisps table and are invisible without it — that is nearly everything
+  a coordination session is ever assigned.
+- The hq/city store is NOT addressable as `gc bd --rig=<name>`, even though
+  `gc rig list` reports it. That form fails with `rig "<name>" not found` AND
+  exits 0, so it silently contributes an empty queue. Read the city with a
+  bare `gc bd list` and iterate only non-hq rigs.
+
 ```bash
-gc agents list --json --active | jq -r '.[] | .agent_id' | while read -r AGENT; do
-    ASSIGNED=$(gc bd list --status=open --assignee="$AGENT" --json --limit=0 | jq 'length')
-    LATEST=$(gc bd list --status=open --assignee="$AGENT" --json --limit=0 \\
-        | jq -r '[.[].updated_at] | max // ""')
-    printf '%s\\t%s\\t%s\\n' "$AGENT" "$ASSIGNED" "$LATEST"
-done
+QUEUES=$(
+  { gc bd list --status=open --include-infra --json --limit=0
+    for RIG in $(gc rig list --json | jq -r '.rigs[] | select(.hq != true) | .name'); do
+        gc bd --rig="$RIG" list --status=open --include-infra --json --limit=0
+    done
+  } | jq -s 'add | map(select((.assignee // "") != ""))'
+)
+
+gc session list --state active --json | jq -r --argjson q "$QUEUES" '
+  .sessions[] as $s
+  | [ $s.agent_name, ($s.id // ""), ($s.alias // ""), ($s.session_name // "") ] as $ids
+  | ($q | map(select([.assignee] | inside($ids)))) as $mine
+  | [ $s.agent_name, ($mine | length), ($mine | map(.updated_at) | max // "") ]
+  | @tsv'
 ```
 
+Any assignee in `$QUEUES` that matches no active session is orphaned work —
+a queue whose owner is gone. Worth a look in the same pass.
+
 The latest bead.updated_at is the cheapest proxy for "did this session
 move work recently?" — every claim, status transition, metadata update,
 or note bumps it.
